# MalloySim scenario: an oriented box dropped onto a ground plane in 2D (M37).
#
# The 2D rigid domain has always collided its bodies as discs. M36 added a box
# collider and box-against-box collision; M37 lets that box rest on a ground
# plane (a Halfplane), the 2D sibling of the 3D box on a plane (M30). A box
# against a plane needs no separating-axis search: the plane has one normal, so
# the box is inside the solid exactly where its corners are, and the four corners
# tested against that normal are the whole story. Up to two touch at once (a flat
# edge), so the contact is a manifold. Since M40 both corners are resolved, by
# sequential impulses that iterate until neither corner's constraint is
# violated (issue #73), and a flat landing stays exactly symmetric: the
# manifold first takes one impulse at its weighted centroid, which for a flat
# edge sits directly below the centre of mass, so it applies NO torque and is
# the whole answer for a level landing. The per-corner sweeps run only when a
# corner is still violated after it, so here none run.
#
# That is the invariant to watch. A unit square is dropped flat, with no spin,
# straight down onto the floor. It must land flat and stay flat: its angular
# momentum stays exactly zero through the bounce, because a symmetric landing
# exerts no net torque, and it never drifts sideways.
#
# The square's inertia is computed from the box and the mass, not typed in. It
# falls, bounces with restitution 0.5 losing energy each time, and comes to REST
# on the floor with its centre at half a side height (y = 0.5), by step 1400. A
# bounce slower than the restitution threshold, 4 g dt = 0.032 here, is
# resolved as resting contact, so the last small bounces end. (The solver
# before M40 had no threshold and left a micro-bounce of vertical speed
# e g dt / (1 + e) = 2.67e-3 for ever.)

type rigid

dt 0.002
steps 2000
output_every 200

# A gentle gravity and a floor at y = 0, its normal pointing up out of the solid.
gravity 0.0 -4.0
restitution 0.5
ground 0.0 1.0 0.0

# rigid_box <mass> <hx hy> <px py> <angle> <vx vy> <omega>
#
# A unit square (half-widths 0.5), centre of mass starting at y = 2, unrotated,
# at rest. It falls, its bottom edge reaching the floor when the centre is at
# y = 0.5.
rigid_box 1.0  0.5 0.5  0.0 2.0  0.0  0.0 0.0  0.0

# Expected, from a real run of the shipped binary:
#
#   step      0   E   8.00000000e+00   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00  0.00000000e+00
#   step    600   E   3.49147400e+00   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00  4.02000000e-01
#   step   1000   E   2.36671650e+00   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00 -2.03000000e-01
#   step   1200   E   2.02260403e+00   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00  1.28250000e-01
#   step   1400   E   2.00000000e+00   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00  0.00000000e+00
#   step   2000   E   2.00000000e+00   L   0.00000000e+00   p   0.00000000e+00  0.00000000e+00
#
# The invariants:
#
# 1. Angular momentum is zero at every step. In free fall there is no torque; at
#    the landing the centroid impulse acts below the centre of mass, so it makes
#    none either. A flat drop stays flat. This is the headline: the manifold is
#    resolved corner by corner without a spurious torque.
#
# 2. There is no horizontal motion. The drop is symmetric, so momentum along x is
#    exactly zero throughout.
#
# 3. It ends at rest at the resting potential: E = m g y = 1 x 4 x 0.5 = 2, and
#    p = 0.
#
# On the ASCII view (an orthographic view of the plane): a single point on the y
# axis that descends and halts, since the square's centre only moves along y.

# Machine-checkable assertions, evaluated against a real run by the scenario
# tests. `step 0` is the state before any step, and the tolerance is absolute.
# The rigid `energy` quantity adds the gravitational potential and is near the
# resting value, so the telling invariants are the two that must be exactly zero:
# no horizontal motion and no rotation, both held through the whole drop and
# bounce, which is what says the symmetric landing exerts no spurious torque.
# check step 0    momentum_x  0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 0    angular     0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 2000 momentum_x  0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 2000 angular     0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-9
#
# And at rest: no micro-bounce, and the square neither sunk nor fallen through.
# The solver stops at a contact residual of 1e-8 v_scale, and at rest v_scale
# is g dt = 0.008, so r = 8e-11 and the square's speed is at most
# 3 r (1 + 2 R / w) = 5.8e-10 (R = 0.707 its corner distance, w = 1 its side):
# the momentum's tolerance, 6e-10. Its resting height sits within the
# projection's slop of the floor, 1e-6 x 0.5, so the energy is 2 to within
# m g 5e-7 = 2e-6, 2.1e-6.
# check step 2000 momentum_y  0.00000000e+00   tol 6e-10
# check step 2000 energy      2.00000000e+00   tol 2.1e-6
# And that it is at rest there, as box_drop.scn checks in 3D (issue #67). The
# 2D rigid domain has no position quantity, so the resting HEIGHT is pinned
# only through the energy line above. Tolerance as box_stack2d.scn's resting
# line.
# check step 2000 kinetic     0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-16
